\section{Storage}{
The storage type used for the encoded values depends on the naxp's maximum
encoded value as follows:
\itemize{
\item less than \eqn{2^{31}}{2^31} is \code{integer},
\item \eqn{2^{31}}{2^31} to \eqn{2^{53}}{2^53} is \code{double}, and
\item more than \eqn{2^{53}}{2^53} raises an error (when a function that needs
encoded values, such as \code{\link[=naxp_encode]{naxp_encode()}}, is called, \emph{not} when a \code{naxp} is
created).
}

Invalid text is \emph{always} stored as \code{0}, never \code{NA}.
}

\section{Missing values}{
\code{\link[=is.na]{is.na()}} and \code{\link[=anyNA]{anyNA()}} treat an encoded value of 0 as missing, which ensures
that the encoded values of invalid text and \code{NA} text are both treated as
missing. In particular, \code{is.na(x)} matches \code{is.na(naxp_decode(x))} and
\code{\link[=na.omit]{na.omit()}} drops missing values.

Beware that code that reads the stored values directly will \emph{not} treat the 0
as missing. For instance, \code{\link[=as.numeric]{as.numeric()}} passes 0 through unchanged, and
\code{\link[=complete.cases]{complete.cases()}} and functions built on vctrs, such as \code{tidyr::drop_na()}
and \code{dplyr::coalesce()}, treat 0 as present.
}

\section{Printing and conversion}{
A \code{naxp_encoded} vector prints as its canonical text, with 0 shown as
\verb{<invalid (0)>}. \code{\link[=as.character]{as.character()}} produces the text, with \code{NA} for 0.

Use \code{\link[=as.integer]{as.integer()}} or \code{\link[=as.numeric]{as.numeric()}} to obtain the underlying encoded values.
}

\section{Subsetting, assignment and combining}{
Subsetting, \code{\link[=rep]{rep()}}, \code{\link[=rev]{rev()}}, \code{\link[=unique]{unique()}} and \code{\link[=sort]{sort()}} return \code{naxp_encoded}
vectors with the same naxp. \code{\link[=split]{split()}} returns a list of \code{naxp_encoded}
vectors, again with the same naxp. Indexing out of range or with \code{NA} gives
0. A \code{naxp_encoded} vector can be a data frame column. \code{\link[=order]{order()}} and
\code{\link[=match]{match()}} return integer indices, and \code{\link[=table]{table()}} counts each distinct value,
labelled with its text, excluding 0.

Assigning into a \code{naxp_encoded} vector accepts
\itemize{
\item another \code{naxp_encoded} vector with the same naxp,
\item text, which is encoded, or
\item \code{NA}, which is mapped to 0.
}

\code{is.na(x) <- i} sets those elements to 0.

\code{\link[=c]{c()}} combines \code{naxp_encoded} vectors with the same naxp.
}

\section{Comparison}{
Encoded values are compared only for equality. \code{==} and \code{!=} compare a
\code{naxp_encoded} vector with one with the same naxp, with text, which is
encoded first, or with numbers as underlying values, so \code{x == 0} finds the
invalid text.

\code{\link[=sort]{sort()}} and \code{\link[=order]{order()}} sort by encoded value, but arithmetic, \code{<}, \code{>} and
summaries such as \code{\link[=max]{max()}} raise errors.
